  "source_name": "https://www.whattoexpect.com/toddler/33-month-old.aspx",
  "source_type": "website",
  "structure": {
    "block_count": 72,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "33-Month-Old Child",
        "text_sha256": "d032ef257c7b7d200eed563cd67c42fcea9c361da72f9442870960d61d67e74d",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Rewards and bribes are powerful tools when it comes to coaxing your toddler to comply, but tread lightly.",
        "text_sha256": "2e3b178488e417be85a9169abc9d41e9732b549efd55741845104b27d25561d0",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your 33-Month-Old Child",
        "text_sha256": "e1a5d271b370eac46edab88461492998c00724b4ffc107f327466d070ac75878",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "“Eat your peas and I’ll give you some candy.” “Come out of the sandbox and we’ll go buy stickers.” Surely there’s no harm in these basic bargains, right? Well, yes and no.",
        "text_sha256": "ba74dd43e12523ebd6fc57860df5c5ff679caeb08a685db11d3fe8a4db1c67d8",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Using incentives once in a while is totally fine, but making a habit of rewarding (or bribing) your toddler can backfire in the long run.",
        "text_sha256": "3f38f3755586dad06bb844eab1e05df409f06505db551a2227f9dc0bac252500",
        "word_count": 25
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Eventually, he may only cooperate if a treat is offered, thereby missing the concept of doing something for its own value (“Peas are good for me, and I make my parents happy when I do what they ask.”).",
        "text_sha256": "8773fcb85092167bb6bb8b443687e81a47a3a9ad40544e17a5450be0f273c6a3",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A better way to motivate your child? Give out plenty of positive praise — for putting his blocks away, wiping up his spilled milk or putting on his own shoes.",
        "text_sha256": "a94b4ecc2a471069f19d3f022ba78bfe3c08bbceecf7b96823110c822cf422bc",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Here’s what else is on tap for your 33-month-old toddler.",
        "text_sha256": "3075762d6c538d26e49e7f2ac01105842029d3590f8d93968f82682138e6c21b",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your 33-month-old child’s development",
        "text_sha256": "dcb80ea30fab70263e8a05e0443d5384f691666c54455bc5915341f6160b14ef",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "At a Glance",
        "text_sha256": "4007536f1ab858815b5f74377d1a44ff31bd2acc98e17bf65e9e39ed107102a1",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Sleeping basics",
        "text_sha256": "0dcc27c80152c55744471ef2e1438841fe778a224654a5647af1b6660a4ccac9",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Toddlers need about 11 to 14 hours of sleep a day, including an afternoon nap that lasts for about one-and-a-half to two-and-a-half hours, give or take.",
        "text_sha256": "ce845031455dec4bc5076693f9db32a40bfc01e21d99c65767930943eb3db25a",
        "word_count": 26
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Feeding basics",
        "text_sha256": "d131509829837718c7b557ed63e489e7685ee22bf584169a10cd21451fa94a66",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "For a toddler, breakfast really is the most important meal of the day: It’s linked with lower obesity rates, improved moods and better school performance.",
        "text_sha256": "414b2d79f2a2c9c14705376524e8cb4f737a5e9b65ce8878d2ea18335f740a7f",
        "word_count": 25
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Did you know?",
        "text_sha256": "3e9b3541c28b5613892911e0055f50c49000dbb334aa8bdf4bc5e8b37258a833",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Some pretty ritualistic behavior usually pops up around this time, so don’t be surprised if your tot insists on lining up his toy cars in a row or organizing his crayons by color.",
        "text_sha256": "6ef517e7c2a0715d3ba613bb2b5a2193480d3e24d4446899723bf65c16624917",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Now that your child is turning into quite a conversationalist, talking with you is important both intellectually and emotionally.",
        "text_sha256": "4b7d45f63784809d9203a865ababf3b6fde0fe32d839863b9a724ec87f3dc58f",
        "word_count": 19
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Start the conversation early and you’ll boost your toddler’s language development and the chances of lifelong communicativeness. The dinner table is an especially good place to chat — and it’s a good idea to start implementing TV-free mealtimes at every opportunity.",
        "text_sha256": "e3ee64529175638510a7b77c1865e033e496e38ab3856f6f902c6058bcbb301b",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Whenever possible, make time to listen when your child wants to talk, and once you tune in, stay tuned in without doing 10 other things (even if you have 10 other things to do).",
        "text_sha256": "d8625bd76663f4f607ba588fd33f9522854beca16da248894f831f244054f0e8",
        "word_count": 34
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Be an enthusiastic audience and gently offer words that might help your little storyteller express himself more fully, i.e. “proud,” “confident,” “disappointed,” “worried.”",
        "text_sha256": "ff310665d06009cffef6f3781be4174f23561e73d27843674f19414885726519",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "One topic to tackle early and often: teaching your child to care about the environment. First and foremost, be a good role model — point out when and why you reuse things like shopping bags and shoe boxes.",
        "text_sha256": "80984afc32481ea7d3f0c1b21e1e16163843d4f807fdeb00027b2d21c7dd0a82",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Let your child help you recycle bottles, cans and newspapers, and let him see you turn off the water while brushing your teeth or turn out the light when you leave a room.",
        "text_sha256": "7c244f13543d013700b94a52886179aa2ae4293ce059bb713334be651a8f4510",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Explain how the things we do affect the earth and the animals that live here, and you’ll go a long way in teaching your little one to be environmentally responsible for life.",
        "text_sha256": "f094455308702eab942cf21ded3f83a814119af16826841cf4eed6efdbeb3db9",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
        "block_index": 23,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your 33-month-old child’s growth",
        "text_sha256": "ea2825cea88f0713b1b9060ef277426cf193c434c9b14bf9c26275abaa628650",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000024",
        "block_index": 24,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your toddler’s appetite may be up one day and down the next, but at this age, his growth will be fairly consistent. Most 33-month-old boys weigh about 25½ to 37 pounds and measure about 34½ to 39½ inches tall. Most girls weigh about 24¼ to 36¼ pounds and measure 34½ to 39 inches tall.",
        "text_sha256": "fa087e486c7078dd85b82ef702865539622fe90a0b28b99c8635f0e0ff5a98ca",
        "word_count": 54
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000025",
        "block_index": 25,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "And if your toddler barely seems to eat at all some days? It’s perfectly normal for tots to go on a “food strike,” where they refuse to eat much of anything (even his favorite foods).",
        "text_sha256": "77a931f3418afb8fb846fc72cdcfe250293eba9551baae2ca28d35b561b366f5",
        "word_count": 35
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000026",
        "block_index": 26,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If that’s the case, try serving up old favorites in a slightly new form — warm oatmeal instead of cold, or dry cereal instead of cereal with milk. It may be tempting enough to coax him out of his slump.",
        "text_sha256": "4af4eb5e2542d66488c7933deeb3046e2a42cf832c2cf6ee7c7b9a292cfb3412",
        "word_count": 40
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000027",
        "block_index": 27,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your 33-month-old child’s health & care",
        "text_sha256": "e085d2cf979977215dde291ec68e272f87732ace892e50beb7c1f6b93a0793fb",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000028",
        "block_index": 28,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "No one likes being sick. But for a toddler, a stuffy nose and sore throat can be extra miserable — not least because it interferes with his play. Here’s what else is going on with your 33-month-old’s health.",
        "text_sha256": "52512a63ae5f05ff431b7776eab25f7c3bc8168f94d65412b7eb812b872036ef",
        "word_count": 38
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000029",
        "block_index": 29,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Sore throats in toddlers",
        "text_sha256": "152f6490df3b9c89536f82504cf0130f67c0daf3489cd4b5c386b2ec04986a69",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000030",
        "block_index": 30,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Unlike a fever or stuffy nose, there aren’t many obvious signs of a sore throat, especially if your child can’t describe how he’s feeling. Still, there are some clues that you can be on the lookout for.",
        "text_sha256": "1deb7bbee3eab60866168ab78acbca95b2afbd8d29242dad5568cca209db474c",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000031",
        "block_index": 31,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Early signs of autism",
        "text_sha256": "6baa7cc0021559690c1f12cfefd6eef846042111339970cd01b82993debd8f0e",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000032",
        "block_index": 32,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The signs of autism spectrum disorder (ASD) usually appear between the ages of 2 and 3. Early identification of the condition means you can seek the treatment your child needs from the start — which will help you and your child better manage it in the long run.",
        "text_sha256": "73bae5012deb141a13e94b6c2c9e006efa71db0962cc53d0ff30ac0548ccf666",
        "word_count": 48
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000033",
        "block_index": 33,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Treating bee stings",
        "text_sha256": "aa0dd733ed31187629097519c3eead2eb34d5db3c24692ec87cf3e351e1a8122",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000034",
        "block_index": 34,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A bee sting will result in some tears, but in most children, the worst of the pain will subside in an hour or two. Children who are allergic to bee stings, however, can develop hives, have trouble breathing or experience swelling in the lips, tongue or face (if that’s the case, call 911 or go to the ER right away).",
        "text_sha256": "15d835394eaa8c897587ea57fef5ce334beb320addd51094231ce1de44ea7353",
        "word_count": 60
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000035",
        "block_index": 35,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Safe allergy medications for toddlers",
        "text_sha256": "75cb8ced99eaf0377a647005b6fd27e24535b7fe4ef4705c70ecb770c8e23b04",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000036",
        "block_index": 36,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your toddler has seasonal allergies, your first line of defense is to keep him away from the offending allergen — pollen, ragweed, grass — as much as possible. But since you can’t keep your child inside all the time (and wouldn’t want to anyway), ask your pediatrician about these toddler-safe allergy medications.",
        "text_sha256": "e88a84579b734e7cee276398687d6e105e36a5162c1be80aaf16fa2e68ca42ec",
        "word_count": 53
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000037",
        "block_index": 37,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "All about toddler glasses",
        "text_sha256": "eece1e0e985d1df17301d22126d93895ea1ca3a70720ff7ac3102c2ecac4d7d3",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000038",
        "block_index": 38,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Vision problems tend to crop up later in childhood or adulthood, but if your toddler needs glasses sooner than that, there are plenty of adorable — and sturdy — frames that will help bring your child’s world into focus.",
        "text_sha256": "652d373d813059456ddd32f81403d6c9bb3ae49f6544a3c4c6ce333ec34a38df",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000039",
        "block_index": 39,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Toddler tips & info",
        "text_sha256": "b488698e03032da968e850a3a2b0c5472ef517198b57e93a959eb2ab92b91907",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000040",
        "block_index": 40,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Day care vs. preschool",
        "text_sha256": "291c4606984657a4a97dee1ba1e2c48ebe8b2bb8afdae747290ccd8872074019",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000041",
        "block_index": 41,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Now that your toddler is nearing the age of 3, you may be wondering whether you should keep (or start) him in day care or enroll him in preschool.",
        "text_sha256": "dad6f27b056b4f9a7ed438e54bf647ea623adf45a546e0620697ce4201b9ad70",
        "word_count": 29
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000042",
        "block_index": 42,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Day care and preschool have a lot in common. Both types of programs are licensed and regulated by the same state agencies. Plus, many day care centers also use an educational curriculum, which can mirror those of a preschool.",
        "text_sha256": "df15fe8490de39831f0dcd1d55ece2a313d0d3e96cb7644a61b635d2a2ff54ab",
        "word_count": 39
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000043",
        "block_index": 43,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The main differences: Preschools sometimes have shorter daily hours, and ask parents to choose between half-day and full-day programs. Day care facilities, however, tend to operate with longer hours to accommodate working parents.",
        "text_sha256": "1a763e592345aadd0e667cac751e39cb4e6d3949b8a028b68d21f0c2769c8e1b",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000044",
        "block_index": 44,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Preschools may also require your child to be potty trained, whereas day cares admit tots in diapers, though may have requirements for moving up to older-age classrooms (usually it’s by the age of 3, if at all).",
        "text_sha256": "350006ccd8c1a6808904b6a813ca7a24c0dec6a33bcbb327bc71983d90153bfa",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000045",
        "block_index": 45,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Can’t decide between the two? Try visiting a few specific centers in your area to see which one you and your child like best.",
        "text_sha256": "534f98e60d1a2a82b8a1e7c1e48db4387b2b7c9409f8f82a2169e69bc6e47c12",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000046",
        "block_index": 46,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Toddlers aren’t exactly known for their innate sharing skills. But there’s one thing that many toddlers don’t mind sharing — their bedroom. In fact, sharing a room with a baby sibling can help children fall asleep easier and teach them valuable lessons about cooperation, communication and problem-solving.",
        "text_sha256": "e47a3e4ad98967c1b9e00bb80163cbf556d4e5f708515fab233149879fb3fdf6",
        "word_count": 47
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000047",
        "block_index": 47,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Still, the experience won’t be all giggles and carefree togetherness. To ease your toddler’s transition from sleeping solo to room-sharing with a sibling, try putting him to bed later than the baby. He’ll feel like a big kid for getting to stay up later, and will appreciate the extra time he’ll get to spend with you.",
        "text_sha256": "69c48a53253a99657e75d0725908c4f0cf38537b44d270cb326a51081d0106d0",
        "word_count": 56
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000048",
        "block_index": 48,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You can also create separate spaces in the bedroom for each child. Keep your toddler’s smaller toys, such as cars and blocks, up higher so your baby can’t reach them, and store the baby’s toys in lower shelves for easy access.",
        "text_sha256": "1b6c739e9de6fcdcd492c13307ed6c3d88d23998d25dbe395b038309c4878370",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000049",
        "block_index": 49,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It can be hard to watch your animal-loving tot start to sneeze and wheeze every time he cuddles up to a puppy or kitten. But just because your child has a pet allergy doesn’t necessarily mean you have to dash his dreams of owning a furry family member.",
        "text_sha256": "18754fae23b29c007c7dba7a35d878c24f34c55994611facbbc2dab81093b820",
        "word_count": 48
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000050",
        "block_index": 50,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Children who have pet allergies are typically reacting to the saliva, urine or dander — the dead skin cells — in the animal.",
        "text_sha256": "67b3545bffd35e42a0275085ac176b207f051be8fedf5f057f09eedf384cab4a",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000051",
        "block_index": 51,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If your child’s allergies are severe, you may have to avoid owning a cat or dog for the time being (though there are other options, like allergy shots), but if his allergies are mild, you might be able to choose a breed that produces less dander.",
        "text_sha256": "2e341d993a7ecc6b0d7a3515c6f655b7f485dfd2215c5722227b4f1b16f4e573",
        "word_count": 46
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000052",
        "block_index": 52,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Sometimes, puppies and kittens produce fewer allergens and the child will get used to them over time, so pet allergies won’t be an issue later.",
        "text_sha256": "efa26a894968bd6d113711f4515e6665219bc190af896fcea9b580c7a95fafaf",
        "word_count": 25
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000053",
        "block_index": 53,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "While there’s no such thing as an allergy-free pet, less allergenic dogs include poodles (including poodle mixes, like labradoodles), schnauzers and the Portuguese water dog.",
        "text_sha256": "f80d58c475a0de28d15121892114eca623fefad54567301a245123e6367db09f",
        "word_count": 25
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000054",
        "block_index": 54,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "As for cats, the rex and sphynx breeds are said to be less allergenic (among other breeds), but this evidence is anecdotal, not scientific.",
        "text_sha256": "183df7943713a96b661ccaaf7abbe8142d2dd7f45f6400d830d67c6e55a7f8dd",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000055",
        "block_index": 55,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "To help keep your toddler’s allergies under control if he has them, try to bathe your pet frequently, cordon off a pet-free zone (like your child’s bedroom and playroom), and use a HEPA air purifier to keep the air clear of dander as much as possible.",
        "text_sha256": "17b1bf7473bea9e5f11918c85bde56d45c409341c529f71c6d100b0988347c1f",
        "word_count": 46
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000056",
        "block_index": 56,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "How to avoid a spoiled toddler",
        "text_sha256": "b028720c71da622c2bbfa0afef83744215c0d91a12a691b9fa8f2b18bacefcab",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000057",
        "block_index": 57,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You can’t spoil a newborn, but you can certainly spoil a toddler — which can set the stage for more tantrums later on.",
        "text_sha256": "556889984b30b8b5fec84293e22c2f5c6888b8eef5292fd8667b85f14be6b07d",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000058",
        "block_index": 58,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "As tempting as it is to give into his demands, try to set — and then stick to — clear rules. Kids will try to push your boundaries, but consistency is key.",
        "text_sha256": "82037624ab7a7e03bc1ee4284a0fe38124749721fa94e82ba174e4aa0d288a9b",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000059",
        "block_index": 59,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If they learn that whining or throwing a tantrum will get them what they want, they’ll continue to do so for as long as it keeps working.",
        "text_sha256": "ad1d44b135d639844e8ad14aaae04465d8e828482b2751df665cacd8436b9fba",
        "word_count": 27
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000060",
        "block_index": 60,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Likewise, don’t give your child a million chances to correct his behavior (“Put the toy down on one, two … two-and-a-half, two-and-three-quarters …”) or strike up a deal with him every time you need him to leave the playground . Otherwise, your toddler will come to expect a treat every time he gets in the car to go home.",
        "text_sha256": "f530f502d6376c5df3548d6124916d2dac58e7d59dfd4d37caf407ba7990a0a4",
        "word_count": 59
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000061",
        "block_index": 61,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "When toddlers touch themselves",
        "text_sha256": "6b7df64944d4512af46dda64b25c804c36c988801a226d675ca16032a2f14df7",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000062",
        "block_index": 62,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Around the time your toddler transitions to training pants — or starts potty training, where private parts become a major focus — he may also take an interest in his genitals .",
        "text_sha256": "70d4ebee7c2f49f057126686307fd8b1383e7438d21b046f4e34d190dd8edb6f",
        "word_count": 32
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000063",
        "block_index": 63,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Rest assured, this is normal — and innocent — behavior. When a toddler touches himself, there’s nothing sexual about it, so don’t shame or scold your child for his actions.",
        "text_sha256": "8a9b370aefc97636950ed434c1ee36f62e8f7c60856fb4321f10bd42a1ef87ed",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000064",
        "block_index": 64,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "At home, you may be better off ignoring it, but in public, it’s okay to tell him that some things are okay to do in private, but not in front of other people.",
        "text_sha256": "35f46ac962664f6675c4bb38fe3df7c6f35190380372c6645cb52fb05fadfb73",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000065",
        "block_index": 65,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Sunscreen 101",
        "text_sha256": "a2f4181c2ce17764a0991583ae9647753b0abfe59eacd121988defd31256d3ee",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000066",
        "block_index": 66,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Your toddler might want to spend the entire day at the park — but he’ll need to reapply sunscreen at least every two hours, even on cloudy days. Sun damage early in childhood can increase the risk of skin cancer later in life.",
        "text_sha256": "364b0316eaaf739d4fed54bdb4bccbb5a74e669fffc0a2828610bf3f739ea5a5",
        "word_count": 43
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000067",
        "block_index": 67,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "About 15 to 30 minutes before your child goes outside, portion out about a toddler-sized palmful of sunscreen (look for “broad-spectrum” brands that are SPF 30 to 50) and apply it to your tot’s legs, arms and face as well as the often-missed areas like the ears, neck, backs of legs, feet and hands.",
        "text_sha256": "0e57b3215de662775175cb4da0ab12779f1ed7bc03783acde8dec16ad16133e4",
        "word_count": 54
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000068",
        "block_index": 68,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Top it off with sun-protective clothing that has SPF woven into the fabric, a broad-brimmed hat and toddler-sized sunglasses with at least 99 percent UV protection.",
        "text_sha256": "ad60a33eec8b38fd63964819875d3d52c49a8d387357c0f3c126320992a45a1c",
        "word_count": 26
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000069",
        "block_index": 69,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Sneaky sources of caffeine",
        "text_sha256": "93c542c7163f04ffedaeb6ce3181a5c442fa32728f262f7acd8cf3e401f2fa99",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000070",
        "block_index": 70,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Young children are a lot more susceptible to the effects of caffeine than adults. In kids, it can do more than just interfere with sleep — it can also cause jitteriness, headaches, tummy aches and trouble concentrating.",
        "text_sha256": "da45d50fe50c0dace568cd9e36bcdd5a0d5ab3bf6c326cace335d580bd55b5e2",
        "word_count": 37
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000071",
        "block_index": 71,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "The stimulant can be found in some surprising sources: sports and energy drinks, vitamin waters and even chocolate. Of course, it’s also found in iced teas and sodas, but you shouldn’t offer those to your toddler anyway.",
        "text_sha256": "61faf3fc7f1b8d761800be5623e82872f9ee57ba6b4e246181f5f01438f0870c",
        "word_count": 37
      }
    ],
    "content_word_count": 1910,
    "heading_count": 20
  },
